In article <15038@chaph.usc.edu> edwardlo@aludra.usc.edu (Edward Lopez) writes:
>One of the features of which I consider nethack to be in most
>desperate need is the ability to specify a list of monsters the game
>should double-check before making you hit, including specification by
>some classes. This feature could consume the current safepet option. A
>list might look like:
>
>tame,peaceful,sleeping,floating eye,acid blob
>
>Given how system speed and keyboard buffers can conspire, I think
>this'd make the game significantly more enjoyable... imagine never
>again dying by the method described above, never accidentally
>corroding your sword by plunging it into a blob, never inadvertently
>waking a nymph by running into her...

It would make the game easier to play that way, but I don't think it
is terribly realistic.  Look at it this way...you are getting impatient
about playing, and so you go zipping through the dungeon.  You are not
being cautious (by using the 'run' feature,) you are simply bashing 
the keys.  Likewise, your character is being reckless and he deserves
to run headlong into a nymph who will promptly rip all his clothes off.
The game will stop you from hitting creatures of your own alignment...
but anything else is open game...

